			<div class="content" id="post-content-1933066">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Widely accepted organizational doctrine holds that past behavior holds no predictive power whatsoever for anomalies;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Er, what? If we really accepted that, we lose. We don't all live in fear that <a href="/scp-131">SCP-131</a> will spontaneously start killing people. If past behavior holds no predictive value, our research is necessarily pointless. Moreover, it's equivalent to admitting that our containment is no better than praying to the gods for rain. The Foundation cannot hold this view, not without relinquishing the notion that it <em>can</em> be a bastion against the irrational.</p>
<p>Also, I really don't see why the Foundation decided to terminate the members of 1659. It's honestly hard to imagine how that can go well. Short of a simultaneous strike on hundreds of thousands people world-wide (obviously impossible), most will survive. This stands a huge risk to exposing the Foundation, will certainly make it look bad, and probably wouldn't even neutralize the anomaly! After all, if it could generate itself once, it could probably do it again. I mean, I know you have a dark view of the Foundation-verse, but this is defying reason in the name of darkness.</p>
<p>I do very much like the premise, and the execution is solid (though I really don't buy the subtle reverberations trope, even if I use it too), but the development just sours the piece for me.</p>

							</div>

								
