														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1933096">
									

<blockquote>
<p>By a vote of 8-5, Overwatch Command authorizes the request. Staff are instructed to begin preparations for neutralization. Object is henceforth classed as a Type K5 Neutralization Hazard; resources and contingency plans as such have been implemented.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See? This is why there should be a halfway point between euclid and keter. Or something past keter. Also, this portrays the foundation as a bunch of idiots, because they're taking on an organization with many times their own influence and expecting to win. They also seem to think it's a secret, when the containment procedures make it obvious that Directorate K has access to information about nearly everything on earth, including, one should assume, <em>their own documentation</em>. What's more, SCP-1659 seems to be able to infect any individual at any time, this implies that SCP-1659 could completely dissolve the foundation by converting every single one of it's members into an instance of SCP-1659γ. All in all, an attempt at neutralization is obviously a bad idea in terms of risk vs. reward, that relationship being: The complete dissolution of the SCP Foundation into a silly parody of itself VS. The neutralization of one of almost 200 keter class objects, and a rather innocuous one at that.</p>
<p>In short, it's a stupid thing for the foundation to try to neutralize this SCP. I don't wan't to downvote, because despite that, it's a good SCP, but I don't want to upvote, because I don't want to encourage the production of SCPs involving the Foundation doing something reckless. So this is a No-vote, unless you do something about those collapsibles, like, perhaps, making it a thaumiel or something. That would be cool. You'd make a great SCP, get my upvote, and, my nearly eternal appreciation.</p>

							</div>

								
					
