					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/xstickman" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(305740); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=305740&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610053878" alt="XStickman" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=305740)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/xstickman" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(305740); return false;" >XStickman</a></span> <span class="odate time_1389487723 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Jan 2014 00:48</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1933721">
									

<p>I agree with this for the most part. I understand why the foundation wants to neutralise this thing because from their POV it must be pants shittingly scary to have this organisation out there, but ultimately there's no actual way to do it unless you can somehow kill every single one of them instantly, and even then that assumes that the anomalous force behind it will cease once all infected individuals are dead, which is a pretty foolhardy assumption to make. The only reason it hasn't just infected the entire foundation is because they've agreed not to (at least, as far as the foundation knows; it could be possible that the SCP simply lacks the ability to do that I guess).</p>
<p>I know that the article basically says &quot;okay guys, try to think up of a way to end this thing&quot; which implies they're aware of these issues and are trying to work around them, and the tone of the article implies that this is going to take a long time to plan, but something about how it's written puts me off. The entire article is basically detailing how widespread, dangerous and downright insidious this thing is, then the proposal for neutralisation adds to that by saying &quot;seriously guys, this thing is, like, whack&quot;, and then it ends with &quot;Yup, let's kill it. Anyway, moving on&#8230;&quot;</p>
<p>Just from my point of view there needs to be at least a little bit of discussion (or something), from a pragmatic view, about just how hard this thing would be to kill, and how dangerous it would be to even try. Theoretically this thing could end the foundation the instant it even suspected that the foundation was moving against it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
